WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.227 --> 00:00:15.528
My new owner was named Mr.

2
00:00:15.568 --> 00:00:21.772
Barry. His doctor had advised him to get more exercise, so he'd decided to try riding.

3
00:00:23.714 --> 00:00:28.157
Mr. Barry was a kind man who wanted to do what was best for me, but he didn't

4
00:00:28.197 --> 00:00:29.337
know much about horses.

5
00:00:30.698 --> 00:00:34.729
He rented a stall at a stable near his home and hired a groom to take care of

6
00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:38.650
me. The groom's name was Filcher.

7
00:00:39.331 --> 00:00:40.791
He was only a part-time groom.

8
00:00:42.232 --> 00:00:46.534
The rest of the time, he and his wife grew fruits and vegetables and raised poultry and rabbits

9
00:00:46.574 --> 00:00:46.994
to sell.

10
00:00:48.355 --> 00:00:49.995
At first I liked Filcher well enough.

11
00:00:51.156 --> 00:00:54.857
He kept my stall clean, groomed me thoroughly, and handled me gently.

12
00:00:56.698 --> 00:01:00.540
But after a while, I noticed my grain meals getting smaller and smaller.

13
00:01:03.372 --> 00:01:04.792
That was strange, because Mr.

14
00:01:04.812 --> 00:01:06.733
Barry had ordered lots of good food for me.

15
00:01:07.773 --> 00:01:09.474
The grain shouldn't have run out already!

16
00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:13.315
Filcher always arrived at my stall very early.

17
00:01:14.895 --> 00:01:16.516
His little son usually came too.

18
00:01:17.716 --> 00:01:21.297
The boy always had a covered basket with him, which he took into the feed room.

19
00:01:22.537 --> 00:01:26.919
After a few minutes, he'd come out again, call good-bye to his father, and scurry off.

20
00:01:29.019 --> 00:01:31.380
I still had hay to eat, but I missed the grain.

21
00:01:32.476 --> 00:01:34.597
It gave me the energy I needed for my work.

22
00:01:36.937 --> 00:01:37.497
One day Mr.

23
00:01:37.537 --> 00:01:39.558
Barry rode me to visit a farmer friend of his.

24
00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:42.339
This farmer had a keen eye for horses.

25
00:01:42.359 --> 00:01:48.420
"Your horse doesn't look as good as when you first brought him, Barry," he said.

26
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.081
"Has he been healthy?"

27
00:01:50.101 --> 00:01:52.381
"I think so."

28
00:01:52.761 --> 00:01:53.802
Mr. Barry shrugged.

29
00:01:55.202 --> 00:01:57.323
"I've noticed he isn't as lively as he used to be.

30
00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:01.904
My groom told me horses are always dull and weak in the autumn."

31
00:02:03.264 --> 00:02:05.505
"Nonsense!" the farmer exclaimed.

32
00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:10.706
"With your light work he shouldn't have any trouble at any time of the year.

33
00:02:11.346 --> 00:02:12.207
What do you feed him?"

34
00:02:13.507 --> 00:02:14.567
Mr. Barry told him.

35
00:02:16.108 --> 00:02:17.428
The farmer felt me all over.

36
00:02:17.448 --> 00:02:24.550
"I don't know who's eating your grain, Barry, but it isn't your horse," he declared.

37
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.591
"You'd better look into it!"

38
00:02:27.271 --> 00:02:31.112
A few mornings later, Filcher and his son arrived at the stable as usual.

39
00:02:32.628 --> 00:02:34.869
The little boy went into the feed room with his basket.

40
00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:37.849
"A-ha!" a voice cried out.

41
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.311
A police officer marched out of the feed room dragging the boy by the arm.

42
00:02:43.931 --> 00:02:47.292
There was a lot of shouting and some crying, and in the end the truth came out.

43
00:02:48.973 --> 00:02:51.453
Filcher had been sending his son to steal my grain!

44
00:02:52.614 --> 00:02:55.134
They sneaked it home in the basket to feed their rabbits.

45
00:02:56.395 --> 00:03:01.116
Mr. Barry was suspicious after talking with his friend, and had asked the police to look into it.

46
00:03:02.272 --> 00:03:05.514
When he found out what had happened, he fired Filcher immediately.

47
00:03:07.495 --> 00:03:09.356
A few days later, my new groom arrived.

48
00:03:10.096 --> 00:03:13.158
He was a tall, good-looking young man named Alfred Smirk.

49
00:03:14.439 --> 00:03:16.740
Mr. Smirk considered himself very handsome.

50
00:03:17.921 --> 00:03:21.303
He spent lots of time on his hair and clothing and always looked his best.

51
00:03:22.503 --> 00:03:24.244
He liked to make me look my best too.

52
00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:30.668
My mane and tail were always neat and glossy, and my hooves were painted with oil.

53
00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:33.589
That was almost all he did for me though.

54
00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.590
Smirk was one of the laziest men I'd ever seen.

55
00:03:38.831 --> 00:03:42.512
He rarely bothered to check my feet or shoes or groom me thoroughly.

56
00:03:43.993 --> 00:03:48.115
He did a sloppy job of cleaning my stall, and it soon began to stink.

57
00:03:48.135 --> 00:03:52.236
"Alfred, the stable smells a bit," Mr.

58
00:03:52.276 --> 00:03:53.297
Barry said one day.

59
00:03:53.317 --> 00:03:56.238
"Do you think you should scrub out the stall?"

60
00:03:56.258 --> 00:04:03.105
"Well, sir," Smirk said in his polite and charming way, "I can if you like, but it's dangerous

61
00:04:03.145 --> 00:04:04.786
to use water in a horse's stall.

62
00:04:05.847 --> 00:04:07.648
It might cause him to catch a cold."

63
00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.209
"Oh!" Mr.

64
00:04:10.229 --> 00:04:10.889
Barry said.

65
00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:12.350
"Perhaps you'd better not then.

66
00:04:13.931 --> 00:04:16.292
But something should be done about the smell.

67
00:04:16.773 --> 00:04:18.494
Perhaps someone should check the drains."

68
00:04:19.894 --> 00:04:21.955
"Good idea, sir," Smirk agreed.

69
00:04:22.736 --> 00:04:24.317
A man came to check my stall.

70
00:04:26.798 --> 00:04:29.179
He found nothing wrong, and the smell went on as always.

71
00:04:31.084 --> 00:04:34.265
The constant dampness began to make my hooves unhealthy and tender.

72
00:04:35.565 --> 00:04:39.326
Eventually my feet got so bad that I couldn't walk over stones without stumbling.

73
00:04:40.787 --> 00:04:43.047
My master became worried and took me to the farrier.

74
00:04:44.508 --> 00:04:46.988
The farrier took one look at my feet and shook his head.

75
00:04:47.008 --> 00:04:52.410
"Your horse has a disease that is caused by standing in wet, dirty stalls.

76
00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:57.231
I'm surprised your groom didn't notice it before it got this bad!"

77
00:04:58.751 --> 00:05:01.445
Mr. Barry was surprised too—and angry.

78
00:05:02.586 --> 00:05:07.110
He directed the farrier to tend to my feet until I was better and fired Smirk immediately.

79
00:05:08.811 --> 00:05:11.554
But this time he had no intention of finding a new groom.

80
00:05:11.954 --> 00:05:16.739
"Having a horse of my own is too much trouble," he told the farrier.

81
00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:20.742
"I'm going to sell this fellow and hire a horse when I need one."
